      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2010 European Association for Cardio-Thoracic Surgery. Published by Elsevier B.V. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "Our study aimed to analyze the predictive value of intra-operative bypass graft flow measurements for long-term mortality."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "A total of 1593 consecutive coronary artery bypass graft (CABG) patients routinely underwent intra-operative bypass graft flow measurements with the transit-time flow meter (TTFM: Cardiomed(®)). The results of the flow measurements and the demographics were analyzed retrospectively."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "The mean follow-up was 3.8 years (0.5-8.8 years) with no losses to follow-up. Overall mortality was 10.1%. The preoperative left ventricular ejection fraction (LVEF) (echocardiograph) was the highest independent predictor of long-term survival (hazard ratio 0.97, p = 0.004) in all groups. The univariate analysis for the CABG I group showed that besides LVEF, female gender (hazard ratio 3.6, p = 0.02) was also significant. For the CABG II group, additive EuroSCORE (European System for Cardiac Operative Risk) (ES) (hazard ratio 1.4, p = 0.0001) and age (hazard ratio 1.1, p = 0.001) were significant. In the CABG III group, ES (hazard ratio 1.2, p < 0.0001), age (hazard ratio 1.04, p = 0.001), IMA (hazard ratio 0.5, p < 0.0001) and concomitant aortic valve replacement (AVR) (hazard ratio 2.1, p = 0.03) were significant, in addition to the LVEF."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "With quality-controlled surgeons checked by intra-operative TTFM, accurate quantification of preoperative LVEF significantly predicts long-term outcome. Effective bypass graft flows failed to predict outcome in CABG patients, regardless of the degree of coronary artery disease (CAD) and concomitant AVR."
          }
        ]
      },
